  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Platychora ulmi is a fungal species belonging to the Didymellaceae family that primarily affects elm trees throughout New England. This ascomycete fungus is known for its role as a plant pathogen, often found on dead or weakened elm wood and bark.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Ascomata Structure",
      "description": "Forms flask-shaped ascomata (fruiting bodies) that are partially embedded in host tissue with a flattened ostiole (opening)",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Host Specificity",
      "description": "Consistently found on elm (Ulmus) species, making the host plant a key identification clue",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Spore Characteristics",
      "description": "Produces two-celled ascospores that are distinctive in shape and size under microscopic examination",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Platychora ulmi is found on dead branches, twigs, and bark of elm trees in moist woodlands and urban settings throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "As a saprotroph on elm wood, Platychora ulmi plays an important ecological role in decomposing dead elm tissue and returning nutrients to forest ecosystems.",
